1	// Tiered builds for the edit loop (docs/LATENCY.md): `brevi run` and `brevi test` after an edit build the
2	// program at opt-level 0 (about 0.35 s instead of 0.85 s for opt-level 1 on bench/latency) and run that binary
3	// at once. In the same call a background job builds the same Rust at opt-level 1 into the build cache, under
4	// the key a plain opt-level 1 build uses, so the next run of the unchanged program gets the optimised binary.
5	//
6	//   - `brevi build` (what you ship) and `--release` are never tiered.
7	//   - Only with the build cache on: with BREVI_NO_CACHE=1 or --no-cache, nothing could pick the
8	//     background build up, so the build is at opt-level 1 as before.
9	//   - BREVI_TIERED=0 turns tiering off.
10	//
11	// The background job is this binary again (`brevi __tier DEST WORK -- RUSTC ARGS...`), started detached with
12	// no stdin, stdout or stderr (a caller reading the run's output must not wait for it) and at low priority.
13	// It builds into its own folder under the cache, then renames the binary into place, so a reader sees the
14	// whole file or nothing. At most one or two run at once on the machine (a lock per slot in /tmp/brevi-tier-UID),
15	// none start with under 2 GB of memory available, and one still running after five minutes is stopped.
16	// Without a free slot no job is started: the quick binary is used, and a later run tries again.
